+ {"id":"CF-141","title":"P4 — an adapter --check verdict counts FAIL rows only, so an UNVERIFIED harness prints PASS beside its own WARN rows (CF-106 cannot be graded from it)","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured at f49d85b with a PATH-local stub opencode that answers --version and advertises no run/exec/serve mode: `bash scripts/harness/install-opencode.sh --check` prints `WARN user_space_binary`, `WARN non_interactive`, then `OPENCODE: PASS` and exits 0 — beside its own banner line `harness: opencode (UNVERIFIED adapter)`. The summary at scripts/harness/install-opencode.sh:37 tests `$FAILED` only (same shape at install-hermes.sh:106 and install-claude-code.sh:43), so a WARN row can never affect the verdict. specs/HARNESS.md:29 says an unmeasured adapter must be marked UNVERIFIED, so `PASS` contradicts the spec status, and the PRD CF-106 cell would read this verdict as harness support. The two adapters also disagree on whether a verdict line exists at all: on this host `install-hermes.sh --check` exits 1 printing `FAIL python — hermes-agent 0.19.0 requires python >=3.11,<3.14; found 3.14.9` and no `HERMES:` line.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Reproduce on the current tree, from the repository root: the row's probe prints two `WARN` rows (`user_space_binary`, `non_interactive`), then `OPENCODE: PASS`, then `rc=0`, for a stub harness whose `--help` advertises no run/exec/serve mode — the adapter's own banner already says `UNVERIFIED adapter`.\n2. Every harness adapter's `--check` ends with one verdict line from a fixed vocabulary, decided by row counts, not by `$FAILED` alone: `PASS` only when every contract row is `PASS`; `UNVERIFIED` when at least one row is `WARN` and none is `FAIL`; `FAIL (n)` when any row is `FAIL`, with exit status nonzero. The chosen exit status for `UNVERIFIED` is stated in `specs/HARNESS.md` (spec first, per `AGENTS.md` rule 1).\n3. Three arms in `tests/selftest.sh`, each asserting the printed verdict by name against a PATH-local stub adapter: (a) a stub with a `WARN` row prints `UNVERIFIED` and never `PASS`; (b) a stub with a `FAIL` row prints `FAIL (n)` and exits nonzero; (c) an all-`PASS` stub prints `PASS` and exits 0. The three arms share one fixture so only the stub's row counts differ.\n4. `scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh` and `scripts/harness/install-claude-code.sh` print that same verdict line for the same three arms — today `install-hermes.sh --check` on a host with an unsupported interpreter exits 1 printing `FAIL python — …` and no verdict line at all, so a caller cannot tell a verdict from a crash.\n5. `specs/HARNESS.md` defines the verdict vocabulary and the UNVERIFIED rule, and the PRD's CF-106 `Verified by` cell cites this row's arms instead of describing them.\n6. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` with the three new arms' `  PASS` rows and no `FAIL` rows of its own, and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the fixed tip.\n7. The commit carries a `ch:trace` marker naming row CF-141, the three selftest arms and the evidence artifact, and its message states `AGENTS.md` rule 2 (every check must be able to fail).","detail":{"fingerprint":"c98e9969616dcd0cb2d1c3b32f666130a679d19498cd2d58e259d311d1c0d349"},"created_at":"2026-11-04T01:48:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T01:48:00Z","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); mkdir -p \"$d/bin\"; printf '#!/bin/sh\\nif [ \"$1\" = \"--version\" ]; then echo 9.9.9; else echo \"opencode cli help\"; fi\\n' > \"$d/bin/opencode\"; chmod +x \"$d/bin/opencode\"; PATH=\"$d/bin:$PATH\" HOME=\"$d\" bash scripts/harness/install-opencode.sh --check; echo rc=$?"}
+ {"id":"CF-142","title":"P3 - the selftest auth-marker arm returned exit 0 once in four runs at one tip, so the CI-equivalent gate is nondeterministic under load","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured at tip f49d85b in a clean clone while four code-factory sibling lanes ran on the host (loadavg 30-36). Run 1: SELFTEST PASS, 88 PASS rows. Run 2, after two board-only boardctl updates: SELFTEST FAIL (1), the failing arm printed \"FAIL auth marker {auth_required:trueish} was not refused (exit 0)\". Run 3 and run 4 on the same tree: SELFTEST PASS again. Board-only edits cannot affect scripts/verify-node.sh, so the arm is not deterministic: it drives verify-node.sh through a curl shim and asserts exit 1 for a marker whose auth_required is a non-boolean string.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Running bash tests/selftest.sh 20 times in a row at one tip produces 20 identical summaries, with no flip of any auth-marker arm; quote the summaries and counts. 2. If a race exists in the shim or the marker file, name the exact step and add a fixture that fails deterministically before the fix and passes after it. 3. Record loadavg beside the 20 runs so load dependence is named with a measurement rather than assumed.","detail":{"fingerprint":"f615e1fd18ee0e7e4e7f94ad257c3781ea5a01bb6fad76811090598b2cc1ba28"},"created_at":"2026-11-04T01:57:49Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T01:57:49Z"}
+ {"id":"CF-143","title":"P3 — the canonical board's append-only ledger was rewritten in place: df0c8fc re-serialised 35 historical event lines and no gate fails on it","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 in the canonical checkout at 2a3d357, by the duckbrain memory lane of tick code-factory-duckbrain-2026-11-04-01-21-33.\n\nAGENTS.md and docs/BOARD.md both describe the board as append-only, one JSON object per line, and the fleet's skill doctrine forbids rewriting a tracked JSONL board through a JSON slurp/dump. Commit df0c8fc (\"docs(pm): re-anchor the PRD milestone and the acceptance plan to the live tip; file CF-141\") rewrote 35 pre-existing lines of .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl while resolving the rebase this checkout was in at 20:51 -05: `git show --numstat df0c8fc -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` reports 36 insertions / 35 deletions against a one-line row addition in tasks.jsonl.\n\nThe content survived. Canonicalising both sides (`git show <rev>:<path> | jq -c . | sort`) and diffing gives a strict subset: additions only (event ids 163, 164, 165), zero deleted events. What changed is the raw bytes of historical lines — the legacy rows written as `{\"id\": 1, \"timestamp\": ...}` (space after the colon) now read compact. So this is byte churn on an append-only ledger, not loss of evidence, and it is exactly the shape the fleet doctrine warns about: a resolver that reparses and rewrites the whole file is indistinguishable, from the commit, from a legitimate line-wise merge, and it leaves no trace that anything historical moved.\n\nWhy it matters even without data loss: every later audit that compares the ledger's bytes, or that reconstructs \"what did this commit actually change\" from numstat, sees 35 phantom deletions in a docs commit; a future real deletion would look identical; and the append-only promise that makes the ledger auditable is asserted nowhere a machine can check. The git-tracked JSONL doctrine exists precisely to keep untouched lines byte-identical, and this repo has no guard that enforces it on events.jsonl.\n\nThe probe is a detector by design (it prints offending commits and exits 0 on the current tree):\ngit log --format='commit %h' --numstat -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | awk '/^commit /{c=$2; next} NF==3 && $2+0>0 {print c\": \"$0}'\nIt prints exactly one line today: `df0c8fc: 36\t35\t.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl`. Acceptance criterion 3 asks for a gate that reds on the NEXT occurrence (a fixture that deletes an event line must red; the current tree must stay green).\n\nNot claimed: which step produced the rewrite. A line-wise rebase resolution, a `git checkout --ours` followed by a re-dump, and a hand re-serialisation all leave the same numstat; the commit cannot distinguish them. The row is about the unguarded class, not about attributing intent.\n\nRelated, not duplicates: CF-116 (a census coverage assertion mints a duplicate id on a mid-run insert), CF-130 (complete rows with no completion transition), CF-081 (hand-typed measured-state block). None of them notices a rewrite of pre-existing ledger lines.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree, from the repository root: the row's probe prints exactly one line, `df0c8fc: 36\t35\t.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl`, and exits 0. (The probe is a detector, not a failing check: its output names every commit that deleted lines from the append-only ledger.)\n1. `git show --numstat df0c8fc -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` reports 36 insertions and 35 deletions, and `git diff f49d85b..df0c8fc -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | grep -c '^-{'` is 35 — the deletions exist at the raw byte level.\n2. The deletions are formatting-only, proven rather than assumed: `git show f49d85b:.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | jq -c . | sort` is a strict subset of the same pipeline at `df0c8fc` (0 deleted lines in the canonicalised comparison, 3 added), so no event was lost and the defect is byte churn, not data loss.\n3. A gate fails on the next occurrence. Either `bash tests/selftest.sh` grows an arm that reds when a commit touching `.coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl` deletes a line, or a check shipped in the repo runs the probe and refuses when its output is non-empty for any commit newer than the one named as grandfathered. The arm must be shown red against a fixture that deletes an event line and green on the current tree.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` still prints `SELFTEST: PASS`, `bash scripts/boardctl validate` still prints `RESULT: OK`, and the board line count is unchanged by the fix.","detail":{"fingerprint":"f03fe892bc16439ef05715f51a3491d40fe0dec1ce05ab77bc9c425340611b7c"},"created_at":"2026-11-04T02:14:15Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T02:14:15Z","probe":"git log --format='commit %h' --numstat -- .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | awk '/^commit /{c=$2; next} NF==3 && $2+0>0 {print c\": \"$0}'"}
++{"id":"CF-144","title":"P3 — the pending closure-evidence rule names one instrument while the board carries three: four complete rows hold honest sha256(artifact) digests in tree_fp and the rule 'must resolve as a git tree' would fail them","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured at d02916e by code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-04-02-20-15 (QA lane). A census of every complete row's detail.evidence entries shows tree_fp carries THREE incompatible instruments: 4 entries declare a per-entry algorithm and their value is the sha256 OF THAT ARTIFACT, which recomputes exactly (CF-091 and CF-092 recompute MATCH at their commit; CF-064 and CF-065 recompute MATCH, though their bundles are ABSENT at their own commit_hash and exist only at HEAD); 5 entries carry a 40-hex git tree oid (CF-055 equals git rev-parse <commit_hash>^{tree}); 37 entries carry a bare 64-hex value with no declared algorithm; 1 is empty. Two pending rows prescribe the fix as 'that tree_fp must be a git tree hash resolvable in this repository's object store' (CF-047 AC2) and gate it as 'whose hex tree_fp resolves via git cat-file -t to a git object' (CF-074 AC1). Applied literally, that rule fails the four entries that are honest and recomputable: git cat-file -t answers 'fatal: Not a valid object name' for CF-064, CF-065, CF-091 and CF-092. A fixer following either row literally would either mis-grade correct closures or force valid artifact digests to be replaced by none:<reason>. scripts/boardctl validate accepts the field today (RESULT: OK with warnings), so nothing distinguishes the instruments. Boundary: CF-047 is that the doctrine does not DEFINE the field and CF-074 is that no gate EXISTS; this is the rule the fix names being wrong about the field's semantics, closed by grading each entry by its own declared algorithm rather than by one must-be-a-tree test. Board census at d02916e for the class term 'fingerprint_algo' in title+acceptance_criteria: 0 rows.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root): `jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-144\").probe' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl > p.sh && bash p.sh` prints `instrument_census: declared_sha256_of_artifact=4 bare_64hex=37 tree_oid_40hex=5 other=1` and prints a `git_cat_file_t=fatal: Not a valid object name` line for CF-064, CF-065, CF-091 and CF-092; exits 0 today.\n1. The closure-evidence check grades each detail.evidence entry by ITS OWN declared instrument: an entry carrying fingerprint_algo is graded by recomputing that algorithm over the named artifact at the cited ref and comparing it to tree_fp, and an entry with no fingerprint_algo must resolve as a git tree. One run proves both directions with fixtures: a row whose entry declares sha256(<committed artifact>) and whose tree_fp equals that digest PASSes, while the same fixture with one byte of the artifact changed FAILs and names the row id and the field.\n2. In one run at the fixed tip, CF-064, CF-065, CF-091 and CF-092 PASS under rule 1 (their declared digests recompute) while CF-052 (tree_fp = sixty-four zeros, no declared algorithm) FAILs with a named finding; the output shows both verdict classes for those five ids.\n3. tests/selftest.sh gains a numbered section that runs rule 2, and the arm is causally proven: at the pre-fix tip the section FAILs naming CF-052, and at the fixed tip it PASSes; the section's command and both transcripts are attached as the row's evidence.\n4. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS and scripts/boardctl validate prints RESULT: OK (warnings allowed) at the fixed tip, and the fix is pushed with `git rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD` = 0.","detail":{"fingerprint":"223b356ea7bb8abad5b520c4d2a4d12735972899a7d8ab53487c645ef2072c47"},"created_at":"2026-11-04T02:25:40Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T02:25:40Z","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); git show origin/main:.coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl > \"$d/b.jsonl\"; jq -r 'select(.status==\"complete\")|.id as $i|((.detail.evidence//[])[]?|[($i),((.tree_fp//\"\")|length),(.fingerprint_algo//\"NONE\"),(.tree_fp//\"\")]|@tsv)' \"$d/b.jsonl\" > \"$d/e.tsv\"; printf 'instrument_census: '; awk -F'\\t' '{if($3!=\"NONE\")a++; else if($2==64)b++; else if($2==40)c++; else d++} END{printf \"declared_sha256_of_artifact=%d bare_64hex=%d tree_oid_40hex=%d other=%d\\n\",a+0,b+0,c+0,d+0}' \"$d/e.tsv\"; for id in CF-064 CF-065 CF-091 CF-092 CF-052; do fp=$(awk -F'\\t' -v i=\"$id\" '$1==i{print $4; exit}' \"$d/e.tsv\"); algo=$(awk -F'\\t' -v i=\"$id\" '$1==i{print $3; exit}' \"$d/e.tsv\"); printf '%s algo=%s tree_fp=%s.. git_cat_file_t=%s\\n' \"$id\" \"$algo\" \"${fp:0:12}\" \"$(git cat-file -t \"$fp\" 2>&1 | head -1)\"; done; rm -rf \"$d\""}
